body:
This adds `ghostty_terminal_vt_write_until`, which writes VT data like `ghostty_terminal_vt_write` but stops right after the first byte that reaches a caller-provided boundary: ground plus start and end of APC, DCS and OSC strings.

The use case for this is that it allows downstreams to create a high-performance proxy that sits between the pty and the terminal emulator in order to implement custom handlers, filter things out, etc. 

The main constraint I had was that plain `nextSlice` must not move. Normal, mainline, unproxied IO must not have ANY impact from this API. And this achieves that.

**ABI breaking change:** I decided to remove the old `ghostty_terminal_vt_write_until_ground` instead of having a shim because its not a commonly used API and `ghostty_terminal_vt_write_until` replaces it.

## C Example

```c
uint32_t want = GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_BOUNDARY_APC_START |
                GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_BOUNDARY_APC_END;
size_t off = 0;
while (off < len) {
  GhosttyTerminalWriteStop stop = GHOSTTY_INIT_SIZED(GhosttyTerminalWriteStop);
  if (ghostty_terminal_vt_write_until(terminal, data + off, len - off,
                                      want, &stop) == GHOSTTY_NO_VALUE) {
    break; // No boundary in the rest of the chunk.
  }
  off += stop.consumed;
  if (stop.reached & GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_BOUNDARY_APC_START) {
    // Content starts at data + off.
  }
  if (stop.reached & GHOSTTY_TERMINAL_BOUNDARY_APC_END) {
    // The ending byte is at data + off - 1.
  }
}
```

**AI usage:** I implemented most of the Zig, AI did most of the C changes, the tests, and the benchmark tooling under my direction. I reviewed the design, the parser state audit. I wrote the docs and this PR message.

